<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Qaradagh</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1828 CE (approx.) · capital Ahar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain khanate north of Tabriz, centred on Ahar, that emerged after Nader Shah's death and was gradually absorbed by the Qajars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Qaradagh, the 'Black Mountain' country between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras</a> river, became a separate khanate after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747 broke up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>. Local tribal chiefs ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahar-iran">Ahar</a>, a town known for the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> Sheikh Shihab al-Din.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate was a land of mountain villages and pastoral tribes, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and other mineral resources. Its khans, of whom Kazem Khan Qaradaghi is among the best known, competed with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> khanates and paid tribute to whichever power dominated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Records of its internal government are sparse.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> brought Qaradagh under its control in the 1790s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> in 1828, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras</a> became the border, and Qaradagh remained within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as a governorate.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-qaradagh.jpg" alt="Khanate of Qaradagh" width="320" height="270"><figcaption>Image: Sword313 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garadagh_khanate_(eng).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1828 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ahar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kazem Khan Qaradaghi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qaradagh Khanate, Karadagh Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=khanate-of-qaradagh" aria-label="Khanate of Qaradagh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5850 796 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Qaradagh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6170 1052L6298 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6170 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6170 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ahar. <a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=khanate-of-qaradagh">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

